data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies organisms' genomes. It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- The Cas9 nuclease, paired with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), is delivered into a cell and cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, unlike early methods that inserted DNA randomly. The cuts are double-strand breaks made by programmable \"molecular scissors\" [3].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences come from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phages in later infections [2].\n- A Hacker News headline links CRISPR to HIV elimination in cell culture, but the source is only a headline and does not show the study's details [5]."}

